%U A399941 13,13,13,13,13,13,13,14,14,14,14,14,15,15,15,15
%N A399941 a(n) is the smallest number of lines needed to cover the points of the first n primes in the Ulam spiral.
%C A399941 The Ulam spiral starts with 1 at (0,0), 2 at (1,0), 3 at (1,1), 4 at (0,1), and proceeds counterclockwise.
%C A399941 Let P(k) denote the position of the k-th prime on the Ulam spiral. Then a(n) is the minimum number of straight lines whose union contains P(1), P(2), ..., P(n).
%C A399941 Lines may have arbitrary slopes, including vertical, and may pass through any number of prime positions. A line covering only one prime position is permitted.
%C A399941 The sequence is invariant under rotations and reflections of the spiral.
%C A399941 There may be multiple distinct sets of lines achieving the minimum.
%H A399941 Peter Howell, <a href="/A399941/b399941.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2508</a>
%H A399941 Peter Howell, <a href="https://github.com/SnarkleLabs/ulam-prime-line-cover">C++ source code for computing the minimum line covers</a>.
%e A399941 For n = 4, the first four primes are 2, 3, 5, and 7, occupying positions (1,0), (1,1), (-1,1), and (-1,-1), respectively. The vertical lines x = 1 and x = -1 cover all four points, so a(4) = 2. No single line contains all four points.
%Y A399941 Cf. A296030 (coordinates of successive integers in this orientation of the Ulam spiral), A214664 and A214665 (x- and y-coordinates, respectively, of the primes on the Ulam Spiral).
%K A399941 nonn,new
%O A399941 1,3
%A A399941 _Peter Howell_, Sep 15 2026
